<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plymouth, Montserrat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plymouth is the abandoned capital of Montserrat, a British territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. The island's main town and port since colonial times, it was buried under ash and pyroclastic flows by the eruptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soufriere-hills">Soufrière Hills volcano</a>, which began in 1995 and destroyed the town in 1997. It remains the official capital, though it lies in an exclusion zone and the government works from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brades">Brades</a> in the north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plymouth-montserrat.jpg" alt="Plymouth, Montserrat" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xb-70 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plymouth_Montserrat_Heli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Plymouth lies on the lower south-western slope of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soufriere-hills">Soufrière Hills volcano</a>, on the island of Montserrat in the Leeward Island chain of the Lesser Antilles. The town was laid out in the Georgian era on old lava deposits, close to a volcano that had been inactive for centuries. For centuries it was the only port of entry to the island, and it held almost all of Montserrat's shops and services as well as its seat of government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the first European colony on Montserrat was founded in 1632, St. Anthony's Church was established in Plymouth in 1636. It had to be rebuilt several times after earthquakes and hurricanes. On 17 September 1989 Hurricane Hugo destroyed a 55-metre stone jetty in the harbour and left many buildings, including the island's only hospital, unusable.</p><p>The volcano began erupting on 18 July 1995, and ash fell across the south of the island. Residents of Plymouth were evacuated in August 1995 and again on 1 December 1995, and for the third and final time on 3 April 1996. The island was divided into risk zones, with entry to the town sometimes banned. On 25 June 1997 an eruption produced pyroclastic surges that killed 19 people. Between 4 and 8 August 1997 further large eruptions destroyed about 80 per cent of the town and buried it under about 1.4 metres of ash, burning many buildings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> helped take the population to safety.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The southern half of the island was declared an exclusion zone. Plymouth, which had about 4,000 inhabitants, remains the de jure capital, while the government works from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brades">Brades</a> in the north. The airport was buried in ash and a new one opened in 2005 near Brades. Between 1995 and 2000 two thirds of the island's population fled, many to the United Kingdom, leaving fewer than 1,200 residents in 1997. The volcanic deposits are hard, similar in density to concrete, so clearing the town was judged too costly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Plymouth, Montserrat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Soufrière Hills volcano erupted from 1995, destroying the capital <b>Plymouth</b> in 1997 and forcing two thirds of the people to leave.</blockquote><a href="/state/montserrat">Montserrat</a></li></ul></section>
